I chose all four state‑feedback weights to be negative because a stable linear controller for the cart‑pole must push the cart opposite to the sign of the pole angle, pole angular velocity, cart displacement and cart velocity. Large magnitudes (around –5) on the angle (params[1]) and angular‑velocity (params[3]) terms give a strong corrective force for the rapidly diverging pole, while moderate negative gains (–2) on position (params[0]) and cart speed (params[2]) help keep the cart near the centre. The bias term is set to zero to avoid a constant offset. This configuration explores a strong, sign‑correct linear policy that is likely to keep the pole upright longer than the previously observed low‑reward policies.
